# Cache rules for Cloudflare Workers static assets.
#
# Files under web/ are copied verbatim into build/web/ by `flutter build web`,
# and `wrangler.toml` serves that directory, so this file ships with the bundle.
#
# WHY EVERYTHING REVALIDATES
#
# The usual advice is "no-cache the entry points, immutable the hashed assets".
# It does not apply here: **Flutter web emits no hashed filenames**. Checking a
# real build/web shows main.dart.js, flutter.js, assets/AssetManifest.bin,
# assets/fonts/MaterialIcons-Regular.otf and canvaskit/canvaskit.wasm — every
# one a fixed name whose contents change between builds. Marking those
# `immutable` would pin a year-old font or an old main.dart.js in the browser
# with no way to evict it, which is worse than the staleness it tries to fix.
#
# `no-cache` does not mean "do not store". It means "revalidate before using":
# the browser keeps the bytes and sends an If-None-Match, and Cloudflare
# answers 304 when nothing changed. The cost is one conditional request per
# file; the benefit is that a deploy is always picked up.
#
# This matters more than usual here because, as of Flutter 3.44, the generated
# service worker caches nothing — it unregisters itself on activate. The HTTP
# cache is the only cache this app has.

/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
